---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ExtractPdfPages from '@/components/tools/pdf/ExtractPdfPages.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="extract-pdf-pages">
  <ExtractPdfPages client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>How to extract pages from a PDF</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Drop the PDF onto the box above.</li>
        <li>
          Click the pages you want, in the order you want them. A numbered badge on each selected
          thumbnail shows its position in the output.
        </li>
        <li>
          To type the selection, enter for example <code class="font-mono">5, 1, 12-14</code> and press
          Apply range. The output uses the order you type.
        </li>
        <li>
          Choose one combined PDF or a ZIP with one file per page, then press <strong>Extract</strong>.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Order matters, and that is the point</h2>
      <p>
        Many extraction tools sort your selection and return the pages in document order. This one keeps
        the order you chose, so you can also use it to reorder a document: put page 9 first, then pages 2
        to 4, then the appendix. For ascending order, click <strong>Sort ascending</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can also select the same page twice, for example to make a repeated separator sheet.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>One PDF or one file per page?</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[560px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Output</th>
              <th scope="col">You get</th>
              <th scope="col">Use it when</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>One combined PDF</td>
              <td>A single file with your pages in the chosen order</td>
              <td>The pages form one document: a chapter, a section, a signed set</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>ZIP, one PDF per page</td>
              <td>
                Files named <code class="font-mono">name_page-04.pdf</code>, zero-padded so they sort
                correctly
              </td>
              <td>Each page is its own document: invoices, certificates, tickets</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Quality and fidelity</h2>
      <p>
        Extraction copies page objects directly into the new document: the same drawing operators, the same
        embedded fonts, the same image streams at their original resolution. Nothing is rasterised or
        compressed again, so text stays selectable and searchable and vector graphics stay sharp at any
        zoom. Link and comment annotations on the extracted pages are copied; document outlines and
        attachments are not, because they belong to the whole original document.
      </p>
      <p>
        To get the pages as images, use the PDF to JPG converter. To make them smaller, put the extracted
        file through the compressor.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
